  {
   "id": "xsp9Qmy-tjIJ",
   "from": "Cody Ross",
   "author": "d1237c2d244e29ff",
   "date": "2013-11-11T22:07:18Z",
   "subject": "How to deal with \"Jacobian of the Transform\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Using <a href=\"https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467516217000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3Rzke2EUr6EUHR1T7d_xU_\">https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan</a> the Weibull Mice example, I have a question about when it is necessary to deal with adjustments to the &quot;Jacobian of the Transform&quot;. <br><br>Imagine that in an application of the Mice model to human survival data, we want to allow the &quot;r&quot; parameters  to vary by cluster (There might be higher infant mortality in some clusters for example), and we want to partially pool information across clusters. &quot;r&quot; is constrained to be positive. <br><br>One way to do this, would be to write:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real muR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sdR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>transformed parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]&lt;-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\">normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">muR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sdR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><pre><span style=\"color:#606\">Blah</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> weibull</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">beta </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></pre></div></code></div><br>In this example, should there be an adjustment to the Jacobian? If so what would it be?<br><br>If the model was instead specified as:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real mur</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>real</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> sdr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\">lognormal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">mur</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\">sdr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br><br></span><pre><span style=\"color:#606\">Blah</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> weibull</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]],</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-</span><span style=\"color:#000\">beta </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#000\">n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]));</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br></span></pre></div></code></div><br>Would there be a need to adjust the Jacobian? If so what would it look like?<br><br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
