    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div class=\"gmail_quote\"><blockquote class=\"gmail_quote\" style=\"margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px #ccc solid;padding-left:1ex\">\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\">Using <a href=\"https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467516222000&amp;usg=AOvVaw0Szj0NAA2ItTdFR3z5_OyK\">https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan</a> the Weibull Mice example, I have a question about when it is necessary to deal with adjustments to the &quot;Jacobian of the Transform&quot;. <br>\r\n<br>Imagine that in an application of the Mice model to human survival data, we want to allow the &quot;r&quot; parameters  to vary by cluster (There might be higher infant mortality in some clusters for example), and we want to partially pool information across clusters. &quot;r&quot; is constrained to be positive. <br>\r\n<br>One way to do this, would be to write:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span><br>parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br>real R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span><br>real muR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\nreal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span>lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span> sdR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...}</span><span><br><br>transformed parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br>real r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#008\">for</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>z </span><span style=\"color:#008\">in</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">:</span><span>Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span><span><br>\r\nr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]&lt;-</span><span>exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>R</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">])</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br>\r\nR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>normal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>muR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>sdR</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n<br></span><pre><span style=\"color:#606\">Blah</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> weibull</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]],</span><span> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-</span><span>beta </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]));</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span></pre></div></code></div><br>In this example, should there be an adjustment to the Jacobian? If so what would it be?<br>\r\n<br>If the model was instead specified as:<br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span><br>parameters</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br>real r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>Z</span><span style=\"color:#660\">];</span><span><br>real mur</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\nreal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&lt;</span><span>lower</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">0</span><span style=\"color:#660\">&gt;</span><span> sdr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">;</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...}</span><span><br><br>model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">{</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span>lognormal</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>mur</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span>sdr</span><span style=\"color:#660\">);</span><span><br>\r\n<br></span><pre><span style=\"color:#606\">Blah</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]</span><span> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span> weibull</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span>r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]],</span><span> exp</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(-</span><span>beta </span><span style=\"color:#660\">/</span><span> r</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span style=\"color:#606\">Cluster</span><span style=\"color:#660\">[</span><span>n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">]]));</span><span><br>\r\n</span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span><br></span><span style=\"color:#660\">}</span><span><br></span></pre></div></code></div><br>Would there be a need to adjust the Jacobian? If so what would it look like?<br>\r\n<br><br></div>\r\n\r\n<p></p></blockquote></div>"
