  {
   "id": "m5_zVuFU3_wJ",
   "from": "Bob Carpenter",
   "author": "8aa9b8cc06fdc99c",
   "date": "2013-11-12T09:33:08Z",
   "subject": "Re: [stan-users] How to deal with \"Jacobian of the Transform\"",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "As Jiqiang points out, I tried to elaborate a bit\r<br>more in the transformed parameter chapter of the\r<br>2.0.1 manual.\r<br>\r<br>I don&#39;t know if you were spooked by the warnings in\r<br>2.0.x, but in our next release (or the one after), I&#39;ll\r<br>try to reduce the false positives and if I can&#39;t figure\r<br>out how to do that reasonably easily, I&#39;ll just turn them\r<br>off.\r<br>\r<br>- Bob\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     2,
     "\r<br>\r<br>On 11/11/13, 11:39 PM, Jiqiang Guo wrote:\r<br>&gt; Stan manual says it is not necessary here as you are not specifying a distribution on a transformed parameter.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt; Jiqiang\r<br>&gt;\r<br>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "&gt; On Nov 11, 2013 5:07 PM, &quot;Cody Ross&quot; &lt;<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ctr...@ucdavis.edu</a> &lt;mailto:<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">ctr...@ucdavis.edu</a>&gt;&gt; wrote:\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Using <a href=\"https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792467516224000&amp;usg=AOvVaw1-aAMWjiQ1vc2Ro0phrI1_\">https://github.com/stan-dev/stan/blob/master/src/models/bugs_examples/vol1/mice/mice.stan</a> the Weibull Mice\r<br>&gt;     example, I have a question about when it is necessary to deal with adjustments to the &quot;Jacobian of the Transform&quot;.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Imagine that in an application of the Mice model to human survival data, we want to allow the &quot;r&quot; parameters  to\r<br>&gt;     vary by cluster (There might be higher infant mortality in some clusters for example), and we want to partially pool\r<br>&gt;     information across clusters. &quot;r&quot; is constrained to be positive.\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     One way to do this, would be to write:\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     parameters{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     real R[Z];\r<br>&gt;     real muR;\r<br>&gt;     real&lt;lower=0&gt; sdR;\r<br>&gt;     ...}\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     transformed parameters{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     real r[Z];\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     for(z in 1:Z)\r<br>&gt;     r[z]&lt;-exp(R[z])\r<br>&gt;     ... }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     model{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     R~normal(muR,sdR);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Blah[n] ~ weibull(r[Cluster[n]], exp(-beta / r[Cluster[n]]));\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     In this example, should there be an adjustment to the Jacobian? If so what would it be?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     If the model was instead specified as:\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     parameters{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     real r[Z];\r<br>&gt;     real mur;\r<br>&gt;     real&lt;lower=0&gt; sdr;\r<br>&gt;     ...}\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     model{\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     r~lognormal(mur,sdr);\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Blah[n] ~ weibull(r[Cluster[n]], exp(-beta / r[Cluster[n]]));\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     ...\r<br>&gt;     }\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     |\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     Would there be a need to adjust the Jacobian? If so what would it look like?\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;\r<br>&gt;     --\r<br>&gt;     You received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;stan users mailing list&quot; group.\r<br>&gt;     To unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to\r<br>"
